1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,837
Chapter 17: Prenatal Diagnosis.

3
00:00:05,837 --> 00:00:26,337
Historical Perspective Cell-Free Dna Screening Analyte-Based Aneuploidy Screening Ultrasound Screening Carrier Screening For Genetic Disorders Amniocentesis Chorionic Villus Sampling Preimplantation Genetic Tests References Prenatal diagnosis is the science of identifying congenital abnormalities and genetic conditions in the fetus.

4
00:00:26,337 --> 00:00:41,062
It encompasses the diagnosis of structural malformations with specialized ultrasound, screening tests for aneuploidy, carrier screening for genetic diseases, and diagnostic tests performed on chorionic villi and amnionic fluid.

5
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:52,162
Based on population-based registry data including births, fetal deaths, and pregnancy terminations, 4 per 1000 pregnancies have a chromosomal abnormality .

6
00:00:52,162 --> 00:01:07,337
If chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA) is performed on chorionic villi or amnionic fluid, an additional 4 per 1000 are found to have a pathogenic copy number variant, such as a microdeletion or microduplication .

7
00:01:07,337 --> 00:01:13,037
It is important for all pregnant women to be offered both screening and diagnostic tests.

8
00:01:13,037 --> 00:01:22,200
The goal of prenatal diagnosis is to provide accurate information about short- and long-term prognosis, recurrence risk, and potential therapy.

9
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:27,175
Nondirective counseling and provision of unbiased knowledge are paramount.

10
00:01:27,175 --> 00:01:42,162
Management of an affected pregnancy that includes diagnostic testing, discussion of potential fetal therapy options and postnatal care, and decisions related to expectant management or pregnancy termination are all incorporated into counseling .

11
00:01:42,162 --> 00:01:52,150
Fetal imaging of congenital anomalies is discussed in Chapter 15, fetal therapy in Chapter 19, and pregnancy termination in Chapter 11.

12
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:54,525
Historical Perspective.

13
00:01:54,525 --> 00:02:09,425
Nearly 50 years ago, Brock (1972, 1973) observed that pregnancies complicated by neural-tube defects had higher levels of alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) in maternal serum and amnionic fluid.

14
00:02:09,425 --> 00:02:23,950
Widespread serum screening began in 1977, after a collaborative trial from the United Kingdom established the association between elevated maternal serum AFP levels (MSAFP) and fetal open neural-tube defects .

15
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:35,737
Screening at 16 to 18 weeks’ gestation detected 90 percent of fetuses with anencephaly and 80 percent of those with open spina bifida, similar to current screening performance .

16
00:02:35,737 --> 00:02:40,625
The terms level I and level II ultrasound were coined in this context.

17
00:02:40,625 --> 00:02:52,487
In the California MSAFP Screening Program of the 1980s and early 1990s, the first step in evaluation of an abnormally elevated MSAFP was a level I ultrasound examination.

18
00:02:52,487 --> 00:03:04,300
This screening examination demonstrated that one third of pregnancies with MSAFP elevation had incorrect gestational age, multifetal gestation, or fetal demise as the etiology .

19
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:08,487
Amniocentesis was then offered to the remaining two thirds.

20
00:03:08,487 --> 00:03:18,862
If the amnionic fluid AFP concentration was elevated, a level II ultrasound examination was performed to detect and characterize a fetal abnormality.

21
00:03:18,862 --> 00:03:27,625
Additionally, an elevated amnionic fluid AFP level prompted a concurrent assay for amnionic fluid acetylcholinesterase.

22
00:03:27,625 --> 00:03:37,525
Because acetylcholinesterase leaks from the exposed neural tissue into the amnionic fluid, presence of both analytes was used to confirm the diagnosis.

23
00:03:37,525 --> 00:03:47,325
However, other fetal abnormalities are associated with elevated amnionic fluid AFP and positive assay results for acetylcholinesterase.

24
00:03:47,325 --> 00:03:57,900
These include ventral wall defects, esophageal atresia, fetal teratoma, cloacal exstrophy, and skin abnormalities such as epidermolysis bullosa.

25
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:14,775
Most neural-tube defects are now detected with a standard ultrasound examination, and the detailed ultrasound examination is the preferred diagnostic test (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019c; Dashe, 2006).

26
00:04:14,775 --> 00:04:26,037
Level II ultrasound is no longer an appropriate synonym for detailed ultrasound examination, because the study now includes a much more comprehensive evaluation of fetal anatomy .

27
00:04:26,037 --> 00:04:33,325
As MSAFP screening was being adopted, the designation “advanced maternal age” (AMA) became popular.

28
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:41,800
A 1979 National Institutes of Health Consensus Development Conference recommended advising pregnant women who were 35

29
00:04:41,956 --> 00:04:47,343
years and older about the possibility of amniocentesis for fetal karyotyping.

30
00:04:47,293 --> 00:04:54,606
The threshold was based on the greater risk for selected fetal chromosomal abnormalities with increasing maternal age .

31
00:04:54,606 --> 00:05:03,656
There was also an assumption that the loss rate attributable to amniocentesis was equivalent to the fetal Down syndrome risk at maternal age 35.

32
00:05:03,656 --> 00:05:06,081
This is no longer the case.

33
00:05:06,081 --> 00:05:08,556
Table 17-1.

34
00:05:08,556 --> 00:05:26,193
Estimated Risks for Fetal Trisomy 21 and Any Aneuploidy According to Maternal Age and Timing of Diagnosis In 1984, Merkatz and colleagues reported that midtrimester MSAFP levels were lower in pregnancies with trisomies 21 and 18 than in unaffected pregnancies.

35
00:05:26,193 --> 00:05:37,831
Maternal age was incorporated into the calculation, which enabled assignment of a specific risk (DiMaio, 1987; New England Regional Genetics Group, 1989).

36
00:05:37,831 --> 00:05:52,181
The MSAFP screen detected approximately 25 percent of cases of fetal trisomy 21 when the threshold ratio for a positive result was set at 1:270—the approximate second-trimester risk at maternal age 35.

37
00:05:52,181 --> 00:06:01,268
This trisomy 21 risk threshold and the associated 5-percent falsepositive rate became standards that remain in use in some laboratories.

38
00:06:01,268 --> 00:06:03,418
Aneuploidy Screening.

39
00:06:03,418 --> 00:06:12,006
Aneuploidy is the presence of one or more extra chromosomes, usually resulting in trisomy, or loss of a chromosome—monosomy .

40
00:06:12,006 --> 00:06:36,568
Of recognized pregnancies with chromosomal abnormalities, trisomy 21 accounts for approximately half of cases; trisomy 18 accounts for 15 percent; trisomy 13, for 5 percent; and the sex chromosomal abnormalities— 45,X, 47,XXX, 47,XXY, and 47,XYY—for approximately 12 percent.

41
00:06:36,568 --> 00:06:41,156
Screening tests are available for some or all of these aneuploidies.

42
00:06:41,156 --> 00:06:50,531
The risk for fetal trisomy increases with maternal age, particularly after age 35 (Fig. 16-2, p. 310).

43
00:06:50,531 --> 00:06:57,931
The positive predictive value of all aneuploidy screening tests is higher for women aged 35 years and older.

44
00:06:57,931 --> 00:07:02,718
Such women account for 18 percent of deliveries in the United States .

45
00:07:02,718 --> 00:07:08,293
At Parkland Hospital, the majority of Down syndrome births occur in this age group .

46
00:07:08,293 --> 00:07:23,681
Other important aneuploidy risk factors include a numerical chromosomal abnormality or structural chromosomal rearrangement in the woman or her partner—such as a balanced translocation —or a prior pregnancy with autosomal trisomy or triploidy.

47
00:07:23,681 --> 00:07:26,168
Figure 17-1.

48
00:07:26,168 --> 00:07:31,443
Trends in the percentage of births to women aged 35 to 44 years.

49
00:07:31,443 --> 00:07:38,006
Data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 2015, 2019).

50
00:07:38,006 --> 00:07:46,331
Broadly speaking, there are two types of aneuploidy screening tests, those that are analyte-based and those that are cell-free DNA-based.

51
00:07:46,331 --> 00:07:56,406
Cellfree DNA (cfDNA) screening is more sensitive and specific than analytebased screening, and it has a much higher positive predictive value.

52
00:07:56,406 --> 00:08:09,518
One prospective screening study for trisomy 21 in 15,000 pregnancies found that the average positive predictive value was 80 percent for cfDNA but only 3 percent for first-trimester screening .

53
00:08:09,518 --> 00:08:21,706
However, analyte-based screening has the unusual benefit that an abnormal result may indicate a genetic abnormality for which screening was not specifically performed (p. 337).

54
00:08:21,706 --> 00:08:31,793
The position of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) is that no one screening test is superior in all circumstances.

55
00:08:31,793 --> 00:08:39,943
No aneuploidy screening test is diagnostic, and prenatal diagnosis is strongly recommended before acting upon a result.

56
00:08:39,943 --> 00:08:45,306
There are selected circumstances in which aneuploidy screening is not recommended.

57
00:08:45,306 --> 00:08:54,493
Following diagnosis of a major fetal abnormality, prenatal diagnosis with amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling is advised.

58
00:08:54,493 --> 00:08:59,843
The fetal risk cannot be normalized with a negative aneuploidy screening result.

59
00:08:59,843 --> 00:09:08,956
Screening results can be falsely negative, and major abnormalities confer risk for genetic syndromes not identified through screening tests.

60
00:09:08,956 --> 00:09:18,918
Analytebased screening tests are not valid in the setting of an abnormality that affects the AFP component, such as a neural-tube defect or ventral-wall defect.

61
00:09:18,918 --> 00:09:28,393
Additionally, all aneuploidy screening methods are less accurate in twin gestations and are invalid in triplet gestations and higher-order multiples.

62
00:09:28,393 --> 00:09:30,906
Aneuploidy screening should

63
00:09:31,060 --> 00:09:38,872
be an informed patient choice that incorporates the patient’s clinical circumstances, values, interests, and goals.

64
00:09:38,822 --> 00:09:49,622
Counseling should be individualized, with the understanding that at least 20 percent of women prefer not to receive aneuploidy screening, even when financial barriers are removed .

65
00:09:49,622 --> 00:09:55,660
Elements of counseling prior to aneuploidy screening are listed in Table 17-2.

66
00:09:55,660 --> 00:10:06,035
The screening test(s) offered and elected may depend on factors such as gestational age, provider location or practice setting, and the patient’s out-of-pocket costs.

67
00:10:06,035 --> 00:10:08,560
Table 17-2.

68
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:13,485
Aneuploidy Screening Counseling Elements # Test Characteristics.

69
00:10:13,485 --> 00:10:22,310
Validity characteristics convey information about how well an aneuploidy screening test differentiates affected from unaffected pregnancies.

70
00:10:22,310 --> 00:10:31,297
The sensitivity, also known as the detection rate, is the proportion of fetuses with a particular aneuploidy who are detected by the screening test.

71
00:10:31,297 --> 00:10:34,247
The converse is the false-negative rate.

72
00:10:34,247 --> 00:10:47,997
If a woman elects a first-trimester aneuploidy screen with a sensitivity of 80 percent for trisomy 21, the false-negative rate is 20 percent, so the test is anticipated to miss 1 in 5 affected pregnancies.

73
00:10:47,997 --> 00:10:53,722
Specificity is the proportion of unaffected pregnancies with a negative screening result.

74
00:10:53,722 --> 00:11:00,860
The converse of specificity is the false-positive rate, those without aneuploidy who nonetheless screen positive.

75
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:12,035
Because aneuploidies are individually uncommon, the screen-positive rate, which is the overall proportion of tests with a positive result, is usually similar to the falsepositive rate.

76
00:11:12,035 --> 00:11:17,985
Aneuploidy screening tests generally have screen-positive rates no higher than 5 percent.

77
00:11:17,985 --> 00:11:24,097
Sensitivity and specificity do not convey any information regarding individual risk.

78
00:11:24,097 --> 00:11:31,860
The positive predictive value (PPV) is the proportion with a positive screening result who have an aneuploid fetus.

79
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:35,110
PPV may be viewed as the test result.

80
00:11:35,110 --> 00:11:39,872
It is usually expressed as a 1:X ratio or as a percentage.

81
00:11:39,872 --> 00:11:48,635
Because PPV is directly affected by disease prevalence, it is higher in women aged 35 years and older than in younger women .

82
00:11:48,635 --> 00:11:56,810
Negative predictive value is the proportion of those with a negative screening test result who have unaffected (euploid) fetuses.

83
00:11:56,810 --> 00:12:09,872
Because the prevalence of aneuploidy is low, the negative predictive value of all aneuploidy screening tests generally exceeds 99 percent (Gil, 2017; Norton, 2015).

84
00:12:09,872 --> 00:12:12,347
Table 17-3.

85
00:12:12,347 --> 00:12:23,972
Positive Predictive Value of Cell-Free DNA Screening for Autosomal Trisomies and Sex Chromosome Abnormalities, According to Maternal Age at Term # Cell-free DNA Screening.

86
00:12:23,972 --> 00:12:33,910
This screening test identifies DNA fragments derived primarily from apoptotic trophoblasts, which are placental cells undergoing programmed cell death.

87
00:12:33,910 --> 00:12:37,997
Thus, the term cell-free fetal DNA is a misnomer.

88
00:12:37,997 --> 00:12:44,422
Differences between placental and fetal DNA may cause false-positive and falsenegative results.

89
00:12:44,422 --> 00:12:54,172
There are three types of assays: whole-genome sequencing, chromosome selective or targeted sequencing, and single nucleotide polymorphism analysis.

90
00:12:54,172 --> 00:13:00,022
CfDNA screening can be performed at any time after 9 to 10 weeks’ gestation.

91
00:13:00,022 --> 00:13:10,147
When it was first introduced, cfDNA screening was offered primarily to pregnancies with increased aneuploidy risk, but it has since become widely available .

92
00:13:10,147 --> 00:13:19,922
CfDNA screening is most commonly used to screen for autosomal trisomies—trisomy 21, trisomy 18, and trisomy 13.

93
00:13:19,922 --> 00:13:30,185
It may also be used to screen for 45,X (Turner syndrome), 47,XXX, 47,XXY, and 47,XYY.

94
00:13:30,185 --> 00:13:39,035
A recent metaanalysis concluded that the number of reported sex aneuploidy cases is too small for accurate assessment of screening performance .

95
00:13:39,035 --> 00:13:46,197
The prognosis for these sex chromosomal aneuploidies differs considerably from that of the autosomal trisomies .

96
00:13:46,197 --> 00:13:52,872
If screening for sex chromosomal abnormalities is elected, counseling should include this information.

97
00:13:52,872 --> 00:13:59,247
CfDNA has the highest sensitivity and specificity of any aneuploidy screening test.

98
00:13:59,247 --> 00:14:15,597
In a metaanalysis of 35 studies of largely high-risk pregnancies, the pooled sensitivity to detect trisomy 21 was 99.7 percent, and for trisomies 18 and 13, 98 percent and 99 percent, respectively .

99
00:14:15,597 --> 00:14:17,535
For each of these

100
00:14:17,692 --> 00:14:27,129
autosomal trisomies, the specificity is 99.9 percent, and the combined false-positive rate is less than 0.2 percent.

101
00:14:27,079 --> 00:14:35,617
If the screening platform includes other genetic conditions, each additional condition increases the overall false-positive rate for the test.

102
00:14:35,617 --> 00:14:44,504
The PPV of a positive cfDNA result is provided in the report or may be estimated from an online calculator or reference table .

103
00:14:44,504 --> 00:14:53,092
Because the risk estimate for each aneuploidy depends on maternal age, such information may be included as part of pretest counseling.

104
00:14:53,092 --> 00:14:59,567
This can help to avoid the misconception that a positive screen indicates aneuploidy—it does not.

105
00:14:59,567 --> 00:15:10,417
Because cfDNA screening has a PPV far higher than analyte screening, it may be offered as a secondary screening test for women who wish to avoid amniocentesis.

106
00:15:10,417 --> 00:15:13,554
Such an approach has important caveats.

107
00:15:13,554 --> 00:15:24,054
If cfDNA is used as a secondary screening test and yields a negative result, the residual risk for a chromosomal abnormality may be as high as 2 percent .

108
00:15:24,054 --> 00:15:31,692
And, if the cfDNA result is positive, the delay in definitive diagnosis may affect management options.

109
00:15:31,692 --> 00:15:38,829
In addition to the aforementioned aneuploidies, cfDNA screening is available for other genetic conditions.

110
00:15:38,829 --> 00:15:48,854
These include aneuploidies such as trisomies 16 and 22, specific microdeletion syndromes, and large copy number changes throughout the genome.

111
00:15:48,854 --> 00:15:58,992
Currently, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) does not recommend cfDNA screening for these other conditions.

112
00:15:58,992 --> 00:16:08,067
Sensitivity and specificity of cfDNA screening have not been established for them, and screening accuracy has not been validated clinically.

113
00:16:08,067 --> 00:16:16,179
Limitations of cfDNA Screening In 2 to 4 percent of pregnancies screened with cfDNA, no result is obtained.

114
00:16:16,179 --> 00:16:20,117
These cases are termed “no-call,” or indeterminate.

115
00:16:20,117 --> 00:16:25,442
With a no-call result, the fetal aneuploidy risk is as high as 4 percent .

116
00:16:25,442 --> 00:16:28,654
As shown in Table 17-4.

117
00:16:28,654 --> 00:16:34,929
this result is similar to the average risk conferred by a positive analyte screening test result.

118
00:16:34,929 --> 00:16:37,479
TABLE 17-4.

119
00:16:37,479 --> 00:16:45,742
Characteristics of Screening Tests for Trisomy 21 in Singletons Low fetal fraction is a common reason for no-call results.

120
00:16:45,742 --> 00:16:53,679
The fetal fraction is the proportion of cfDNA in the maternal circulation that is placental rather than maternal in origin.

121
00:16:53,679 --> 00:17:04,204
The fetal fraction approximates 10 percent in the late first trimester, increasing slightly thereafter (Ashoor, 2013; Scott, 2018).

122
00:17:04,204 --> 00:17:19,279
Low fetal fraction is usually defined as <4 percent of the total, and it is more prevalent before 10 weeks’ gestation and in the setting of maternal obesity (Ashoor, 2013; Juul, 2020; Scott, 2018).

123
00:17:19,279 --> 00:17:27,379
The no-call rate may exceed 10 to 20 percent in women whose body mass index is 40 kg/m2 or greater .

124
00:17:27,379 --> 00:17:38,679
Low fetal fraction is also more common in the setting of aneuploidy, particularly trisomies 18 and 13 (Norton, 2015; Pergament, 2014).

125
00:17:38,679 --> 00:17:49,267
The American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics recommends that the fetal fraction be included in cfDNA reports because it assists with interpretation of results .

126
00:17:49,267 --> 00:17:57,342
Pretest counseling should include the possibility of a no-call result and its clinical significance (see Table 17-2.

127
00:17:57,342 --> 00:18:07,354
If the cfDNA screening test is positive or yields a no-call result, additional genetic counseling is indicated, and amniocentesis should be offered.

128
00:18:07,354 --> 00:18:20,092
If cfDNA screening is repeated, the risk for repeat screen failure approximates 40 percent (Dar, 2014; Quezada, 2015; Rolnik, 2018).

129
00:18:20,092 --> 00:18:32,467
Detailed ultrasound examination may be offered, but it is not a substitute for amniocentesis, and the residual aneuploidy risk following normal detailed ultrasound examination is unknown.

130
00:18:32,467 --> 00:18:38,754
It is important to remember that cfDNA results may not reflect the fetal DNA complement.

131
00:18:38,754 --> 00:18:46,592
Rather, a positive result may be caused by confined placental mosaicism or early demise of an aneuploid co-twin.

132
00:18:46,592 --> 00:19:01,417
Alternately, it may indicate maternal aneuploidy, mosaicism, or even occult maternal malignancy (Bianchi, 2015; Curnow, 2015; Grati, 2014; Wang, 2014).

133
00:19:01,417 --> 00:19:08,479
If ultrasound identifies an empty second gestational sac, cfDNA screening is not recommended.

134
00:19:08,479 --> 00:19:13,017
The patient should be informed of such limitations prior to screening.

135
00:19:13,017 --> 00:19:14,717
#

136
00:19:14,884 --> 00:19:17,771
Analyte-based Aneuploidy Screening.

137
00:19:17,721 --> 00:19:28,209
The three categories of multiple marker-serum analyte screening are firsttrimester screens, second-trimester screens, and combined first- and secondtrimester screens.

138
00:19:28,209 --> 00:19:39,896
The serum concentration of each analyte is converted to a multiple of the median (MoM) of the unaffected population by adjusting for maternal age, maternal weight, and gestational age.

139
00:19:39,896 --> 00:19:53,284
The serum AFP concentration is further adjusted for maternal race and for presence of diabetes, which affect calculation of neural-tube defect risk (Greene, 1988; Huttly, 2004).

140
00:19:53,284 --> 00:20:02,134
The analyte-based screening result is based on a composite likelihood ratio, and the maternal age-related risk is multiplied by this ratio.

141
00:20:02,134 --> 00:20:10,546
Adjustment normalizes the distribution of analyte levels and permits comparison of results from different laboratories and populations.

142
00:20:10,546 --> 00:20:21,509
Each woman is provided with a specific risk for trisomy 21 and for trisomy 18—or in the first trimester, for trisomy 18 or 13 in some cases.

143
00:20:21,509 --> 00:20:27,846
Each screening test has a predetermined value at or above which it is deemed “positive” or abnormal.

144
00:20:27,846 --> 00:20:40,484
For second-trimester tests, this threshold has traditionally been set at the risk for fetal Down syndrome at a maternal age of 35 years—approximately 1 in 270 in the second trimester.

145
00:20:40,484 --> 00:20:47,934
The trisomy 21 risk may be further modified based on selected ultrasound markers (p. 340).

146
00:20:47,934 --> 00:20:56,984
A benefit of analyte screening is that pregnancies with abnormal results may have genetic abnormalities other than those for which screening was performed.

147
00:20:56,984 --> 00:21:13,159
For example, in a review of more than 2500 pregnancies with genetic abnormalities from the California Prenatal Screening Program, combined first- and second-trimester screening results were abnormal in 93 percent with trisomies 21 and 18 .

148
00:21:13,159 --> 00:21:29,046
However, results were also abnormal in 80 percent with trisomy 13, in 80 percent with 45,X, in nearly 60 percent with other sex chromosomal abnormalities, and in more than 50 percent with other genetic abnormalities.

149
00:21:29,046 --> 00:21:47,259
Alamillo and associates (2013) similarly identified an abnormal karyotype other than trisomies 21, 18, or 13 in 2 percent of pregnancies with abnormal first-trimester screening results, accounting for 30 percent of abnormal karyotypes in the series.

150
00:21:47,259 --> 00:21:55,784
Women younger than 35 are at lower risk for the specific autosomal trisomies for which cfDNA screening is typically performed.

151
00:21:55,784 --> 00:22:15,159
Thus, if the goal is to select a screening test that will identify the highest proportion of fetuses with any chromosomal abnormality, the yield may be comparable or even slightly higher with integrated or sequential screening than with current cfDNA screening (Baer, 2015; Norton, 2014).

152
00:22:15,159 --> 00:22:29,634
First-trimester Aneuploidy Screening This test includes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), pregnancyassociated plasma protein A (PAPP-A), and ultrasound measurement of the nuchal translucency (NT).

153
00:22:29,634 --> 00:22:33,834
It is performed between 11 and 14 weeks’ gestation.

154
00:22:33,834 --> 00:22:41,109
Pregnancies with fetal trisomy 21 are characterized by higher levels of hCG and lower PAPP-A.

155
00:22:41,109 --> 00:22:50,384
With trisomy 18 and trisomy 13, levels of both analytes are lower (Cuckle, 2000; Malone, 2005b).

156
00:22:50,384 --> 00:22:52,759
Nuchal Translucency.

157
00:22:52,759 --> 00:23:01,734
This is the maximum thickness of the subcutaneous translucent area between the skin and soft tissue overlying the fetal spine at the back of the neck .

158
00:23:01,734 --> 00:23:15,309
It is measured in the sagittal plane and is valid when the crown-rump length (CRL) measurement is between 38 to 45 mm and 84 mm, with the lower limit varying according to the laboratory.

159
00:23:15,309 --> 00:23:20,971
Measurement criteria are listed in Table 14–4 (p. 250).

160
00:23:20,971 --> 00:23:27,459
The NT should be imaged and measured with a high degree of precision for aneuploidy detection to be accurate.

161
00:23:27,459 --> 00:23:33,796
This has led to standardized training, certification, and ongoing quality review programs.

162
00:23:33,796 --> 00:23:36,246
Figure 17-2.

163
00:23:36,246 --> 00:23:44,484
Sagittal image of a normal, 12-week fetus shows correct caliper placement (+) for nuchal translucency measurement.

164
00:23:44,484 --> 00:23:48,546
The fetal nasal bone and overlying skin are indicated.

165
00:23:48,546 --> 00:23:57,346
The nasal tip and the 3rd and 4th ventricles (asterisk), which are other landmarks that should be visible in the nasal bone image, also are shown.

166
00:23:57,346 --> 00:24:01,309
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Michael Zaretsky.

167
00:24:01,468 --> 00:24:07,818
An increased NT is not a fetal abnormality but rather a marker that confers increased risk.

168
00:24:07,768 --> 00:24:20,468
It is helpful to differentiate from cystic hygroma, which appears sonographically as a septated hypoechoic space behind the neck that extends down the back (Fig. 15-30, p. 286).

169
00:24:20,468 --> 00:24:27,468
The fetal aneuploidy risk is five times greater with first-trimester cystic hygroma than with increased NT .

170
00:24:27,468 --> 00:24:40,005
An increased NT measurement is associated with other genetic syndromes and various birth defects, especially fetal cardiac abnormalities (Clur 2008; Simpson, 2007).

171
00:24:40,005 --> 00:24:49,880
If the NT measurement is at least 3 mm or reaches the 99th percentile for CRL, a detailed ultrasound examination should be offered.

172
00:24:49,880 --> 00:25:10,180
Similarly, fetal echocardiography may be considered if the NT measurement is at least 3 mm and is recommended if the NT measurement is 3.5 mm or greater (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020e; American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2019).

173
00:25:10,180 --> 00:25:24,318
As an isolated marker, NT detects only 70 percent of trisomy 21 fetuses, and for this reason it is not used to screen singleton pregnancies (Alldred, 2017; Malone, 2005b).

174
00:25:24,318 --> 00:25:40,543
If cfDNA screening has already been performed, the NT measurement does not improve fetal aneuploidy detection and is not recommended for this purpose (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020e; Reiff, 2016).

175
00:25:40,543 --> 00:25:52,080
NT evaluation may be helpful in multifetal gestations because serum screening is less accurate, may not be available, and cannot provide information specific to each fetus.

176
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:56,418
The NT distribution is similar in twins and singletons .

177
00:25:56,418 --> 00:25:59,443
Efficacy of First-trimester Screening.

178
00:25:59,443 --> 00:26:09,493
Before first-trimester screening became widely adopted, four large prospective trials were conducted, together including more than 100,000 pregnancies .

179
00:26:09,493 --> 00:26:16,168
When the screen-positive rate was set at 5 percent, trisomy 21 detection was 84 percent.

180
00:26:16,168 --> 00:26:23,030
A recent metaanalysis also reported detection of 87 percent of trisomy 21 fetuses .

181
00:26:23,030 --> 00:26:32,030
First-trimester screening similarly detects approximately 80 percent of fetuses with trisomy 18 and 50 percent with trisomy 13 .

182
00:26:32,030 --> 00:26:37,130
Maternal age affects the performance of first-trimester aneuploidy screening.

183
00:26:37,130 --> 00:26:49,768
In women younger than age 35 at delivery, fetal trisomy 21 detection ranges from 67 to 75 percent (Malone, 2005b; Wapner, 2003).

184
00:26:49,768 --> 00:27:00,380
Among women older than 35 at delivery, detection is as high as 90 to 95 percent, albeit at a higher false-positive rate of 15 to 22 percent.

185
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:07,993
In twin pregnancies, serum free β-hCG and PAPP-A levels are approximately double the singleton values .

186
00:27:07,993 --> 00:27:14,518
Even with specific curves, a normal dichorionic co-twin will tend to normalize screening results.

187
00:27:14,518 --> 00:27:19,168
Thus the aneuploidy detection rate is at least 15-percent lower .

188
00:27:19,168 --> 00:27:25,568
Analyte abnormalities are termed unexplained if prenatal diagnostic testing results are normal.

189
00:27:25,568 --> 00:27:43,055
There is a significant association between serum PAPP-A levels below the 5th percentile and preterm birth, growth restriction, preeclampsia, and fetal demise (Cignini, 2016; Dugoff, 2004; Jelliffe-Pawlowski, 2015).

190
00:27:43,055 --> 00:27:49,043
Similarly, low levels of free β-hCG have been associated with fetal demise .

191
00:27:49,043 --> 00:27:55,830
The sensitivity and PPVs of these markers are too low to make them clinically useful as screening tests .

192
00:27:55,830 --> 00:28:06,705
Second-trimester Aneuploidy Screening The quadruple marker or “quad” screening test is performed from 15 through 20 or 21 weeks’ gestation depending on the laboratory.

193
00:28:06,705 --> 00:28:17,905
Pregnancies with fetal trisomy 21 are characterized by lower MSAFP, higher hCG, lower unconjugated estriol, and higher dimeric inhibin levels.

194
00:28:17,905 --> 00:28:31,430
In large prospective trials, trisomy 21 detection was 81 to 83 percent at a 5-percent screenpositive rate (Malone, 2005b; Wald, 1996, 2003).

195
00:28:31,430 --> 00:28:41,993
In cases of trisomy 18, levels of MSAFP, hCG, and unconjugated estriol are all decreased, and inhibin is not part of the calculation.

196
00:28:41,993 --> 00:28:50,230
Trisomy 18 detection is similar to that for trisomy 21, but the false-positive rate is just 0.5 percent .

197
00:28:50,230 --> 00:28:59,430
As with first-trimester screening, aneuploidy detection rates are lower in younger women and higher in women older than 35 years at delivery.

198
00:28:59,430 --> 00:29:04,930
If second-trimester serum screening is used in twin pregnancies, aneuploidy

199
00:29:05,092 --> 00:29:08,204
detection rates are significantly lower .

200
00:29:08,154 --> 00:29:17,329
Quadruple-marker screening offers no benefit over first-trimester screening from the standpoint of trisomy 21 or trisomy 18 detection.

201
00:29:17,329 --> 00:29:28,217
As a stand-alone test, it is generally used if women do not begin care until the second trimester or if first-trimester screening and cfDNA screening are not available.

202
00:29:28,217 --> 00:29:37,592
In 2019, women who initiated prenatal care beyond the first trimester made up more than 20 percent of pregnancies in the United States .

203
00:29:37,592 --> 00:29:44,767
As subsequently discussed, combining first- and secondtrimester screening yields greater aneuploidy detection.

204
00:29:44,767 --> 00:29:49,629
Maternal Serum AFP Elevation: Neural-tube Defect Screening.

205
00:29:49,629 --> 00:29:55,929
AFP is the major protein in fetal serum, analogous to albumin in a child or adult.

206
00:29:55,929 --> 00:30:07,579
Defects in fetal integument, such as neural-tube and ventral-wall defects, permit AFP to leak into the amnionic fluid and result in dramatically increased maternal serum levels.

207
00:30:07,579 --> 00:30:16,804
Accurate gestational age assessment is imperative because the AFP value rises by approximately 15 percent per week during the screening window .

208
00:30:16,804 --> 00:30:38,804
Using an MSAFP level of 2.5 MoM as the upper limit of normal, the neural-tube defect detection rate approximates 95 percent for anencephaly and 80 percent for spina bifida, with a screen-positive rate of 1 to 3 percent (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019c; Palomaki, 2019).

209
00:30:38,804 --> 00:30:42,767
The overall PPV approximates just 2 percent.

210
00:30:42,767 --> 00:30:50,054
Higher screening threshold values are used in twin pregnancies, which have twice the AFP level of singletons .

211
00:30:50,054 --> 00:31:10,829
Importantly, because nearly all cases of anencephaly and most with spina bifida are detected during the standard second-trimester ultrasound examination, MSAFP screening is considered optional (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019c; Dashe, 2006; Norem, 2005).

212
00:31:10,829 --> 00:31:20,567
If the serum AFP is elevated, a detailed ultrasound examination is recommended, and characteristic sonographic findings are diagnostic .

213
00:31:20,567 --> 00:31:25,554
Other abnormalities and conditions also may result in MSAFP elevation .

214
00:31:25,554 --> 00:31:38,979
Amniocentesis for measurement of amnionic fluid AFP and acetylcholinesterase levels can help to diagnose whether a myelomeningocele is open or closed, which is relevant when considering fetal surgical repair .

215
00:31:38,979 --> 00:31:41,617
Table 17-5.

216
00:31:41,617 --> 00:31:49,254
Conditions Associated with MSAFP Level Elevation Most pregnancies with MSAFP elevation have no fetal abnormality.

217
00:31:49,254 --> 00:32:00,117
The patient should receive counseling about the benefits and limitations of detailed ultrasound examination for the diagnosis of neural-tube defects and other potential etiologies.

218
00:32:00,117 --> 00:32:07,792
These include other fetal abnormalities, placental abnormalities, and selected adverse pregnancy outcomes .

219
00:32:07,792 --> 00:32:14,367
The likelihood of any abnormality or adverse outcome increases in proportion to the AFP level.

220
00:32:14,367 --> 00:32:24,992
There are similarly significant associations between second-trimester elevation of either hCG or dimeric inhibin alpha levels and adverse pregnancy outcomes.

221
00:32:24,992 --> 00:32:30,042
The likelihood of adverse outcome is greater if multiple analytes are elevated .

222
00:32:30,042 --> 00:32:37,754
The sensitivity and positive predictive values of these markers are considered too low to be useful for screening or management.

223
00:32:37,754 --> 00:32:44,892
No specific program of maternal or fetal surveillance has been found to favorably affect pregnancy outcomes .

224
00:32:44,892 --> 00:32:47,817
Low Maternal Serum Estriol Level.

225
00:32:47,817 --> 00:32:55,992
A maternal serum estriol level less than 0.25 MoM has been associated with two uncommon but important conditions.

226
00:32:55,992 --> 00:33:04,592
Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome is an autosomal recessive condition resulting from mutations in the 7-dehydrocholesterol reductase gene.

227
00:33:04,592 --> 00:33:15,042
It is characterized by abnormalities of the central nervous system, heart, kidney, and extremities; with ambiguous genitalia; and with fetal-growth restriction.

228
00:33:15,042 --> 00:33:28,304
Detailed sonography should be offered if an unconjugated estriol level is <0.25 MoM (American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, 2019; Dugoff, 2010).

229
00:33:28,304 --> 00:33:34,504
An elevated amnionic fluid 7-dehydrocholesterol level can confirm the diagnosis.

230
00:33:34,504 --> 00:33:40,567
The second condition is steroid sulfatase deficiency, also known as Xlinked ichthyosis.

231
00:33:40,567 --> 00:33:47,592
It is typically an isolated condition, but it may also occur in the setting of a contiguous gene deletion syndrome .

232
00:33:47,592 --> 00:33:49,229
In such

233
00:33:49,396 --> 00:33:56,821
cases, it may be associated with Kallmann syndrome, chondrodysplasia punctata, and/or mental retardation .

234
00:33:56,771 --> 00:34:11,496
If the estriol level is <0.25 MoM and the fetus appears to be male, chromosomal microarray analysis or fluorescence in situ hybridization to assess the steroid sulfatase locus on the X-chromosome may be considered.

235
00:34:11,496 --> 00:34:20,921
Integrated and Sequential Screening If first-trimester screening is combined with second-trimester screening, aneuploidy detection is significantly improved.

236
00:34:20,921 --> 00:34:28,771
Combined screening test options require that specimens are obtained at the correct gestational age and sent to the same laboratory.

237
00:34:28,771 --> 00:34:39,471
The first- and second-trimester components cannot be performed independently, because the false-positive rate would be higher and providing accurate risk assessment would be problematic.

238
00:34:39,471 --> 00:34:47,796
Three types of screening strategies are available: 1. Integrated screening combines results of first- and second-trimester tests.

239
00:34:47,796 --> 00:34:59,208
This includes a combined measurement of fetal NT and serum analyte levels at 11 to 14 weeks’ gestation plus quadruple markers at approximately 15 to 21 weeks.

240
00:34:59,208 --> 00:35:03,908
Trisomy 21 detection is 94 to 96 percent .

241
00:35:03,908 --> 00:35:16,946
2. Sequential screening involves informing the patient of the results after first-trimester screening, with plan to offer prenatal diagnostic testing if the calculated risk value lies above a specified threshold.

242
00:35:16,946 --> 00:35:28,121
There are two testing strategies in this category: • With stepwise sequential screening, women whose first-trimester risk is at or below the threshold receive second-trimester screening.

243
00:35:28,121 --> 00:35:46,358
Using data from the First- and Second-Trimester Evaluation of Risk trial, when the first-trimester threshold is set at approximately 1:30, and the overall threshold is set at 1:270, stepwise sequential screening resulted in a 92percent trisomy 21 detection rate .

244
00:35:46,358 --> 00:35:49,633
The false-positive rate remained 5 percent .

245
00:35:49,633 --> 00:35:56,833
With contingent sequential screening, women are divided into high-, moderate-, and low-risk groups.

246
00:35:56,833 --> 00:36:05,571
Those at highest risk for trisomy 21— for example, risk >1:30, are counseled and offered diagnostic testing.

247
00:36:05,571 --> 00:36:19,971
Women at moderate risk, between 1:30 and 1:1500, undergo secondtrimester screening, whereas those at lowest risk of <1:1500 receive negative screening test results and have no further testing .

248
00:36:19,971 --> 00:36:27,658
Using this strategy, more than 75 percent of those screened are provided with reassuring results almost immediately.

249
00:36:27,658 --> 00:36:35,771
Assuming that women accept diagnostic testing when informed that the risk is elevated, detection approaches 91 percent.

250
00:36:35,771 --> 00:36:38,433
# Ultrasound Screening.

251
00:36:38,433 --> 00:36:52,071
Ultrasound can improve aneuploidy screening by providing accurate gestational age assessment, by detecting multifetal gestations, and by identifying major structural abnormalities and minor aneuploidy markers.

252
00:36:52,071 --> 00:37:02,971
With rare exception, the aneuploidy risk associated with any major abnormality is high enough to recommend prenatal diagnosis with chromosomal microarray analysis.

253
00:37:02,971 --> 00:37:12,696
We do not recommend aneuploidy screening after detection of a major abnormality, because of the many genetic conditions are not detectable with screening tests.

254
00:37:12,696 --> 00:37:17,833
Counseling should include the association of the abnormality with aneuploidy .

255
00:37:17,833 --> 00:37:27,721
A caveat is that fetal abnormalities are frequently not isolated, and associated-butundetectable abnormalities may greatly affect prognosis.

256
00:37:27,721 --> 00:37:30,371
Table 17-6.

257
00:37:30,371 --> 00:37:42,321
Aneuploidy Risk Associated with Selected Major Fetal Anomalies Importantly, fetuses with trisomy 21 and other genetic syndromes may not have obvious ultrasound abnormalities.

258
00:37:42,321 --> 00:37:57,283
Just one third of trisomy 21 fetuses have a major abnormality identified with second-trimester ultrasound evaluations (Bromley, 2002; Hussamy, 2019; Vintileos, 1995).

259
00:37:57,283 --> 00:38:08,471
If major anomalies and minor aneuploidy markers are considered, 50 to 75 percent of pregnancies affected by Down syndrome are found to have a sonographic abnormality.

260
00:38:08,471 --> 00:38:19,258
This is not unexpected, because nearly 40 percent of infants with trisomy 21 do not have major organ system abnormalities identified in the newborn period .

261
00:38:19,258 --> 00:38:28,683
In contrast, the vast majority of fetuses with trisomies 18 and 13 and with triploidy do have ultrasound abnormalities detectable in the

262
00:38:28,852 --> 00:38:30,939
second trimester.

263
00:38:30,889 --> 00:38:45,352
Aneuploidy Markers For more than three decades, investigators have recognized that ultrasound detection of aneuploidy, particularly trisomy 21, may be improved by minor markers that are collectively referred to as “soft signs.

264
00:38:45,352 --> 00:38:56,764
Minor markers are normal variants rather than fetal abnormalities, and in the absence of aneuploidy or an associated abnormality, they do not significantly affect prognosis.

265
00:38:56,764 --> 00:39:05,077
One or more markers are present in at least 10 percent of pregnancies (Bromley, 2002; Nyberg, 2003).

266
00:39:05,077 --> 00:39:11,189
Examples are listed in Table 17-7 and depicted in Figure 17-3.

267
00:39:11,189 --> 00:39:17,964
A minor marker is an indication for a detailed survey of fetal anatomy to determine if it is isolated .

268
00:39:17,964 --> 00:39:20,602
Table 17-7.

269
00:39:20,602 --> 00:39:28,114
First- and Second-Trimester Ultrasound Markers Associated with Fetal Trisomy 21 Figure 17-3.

270
00:39:28,114 --> 00:39:34,052
Minor sonographic markers that are associated with increased risk for fetal Down syndrome.

271
00:39:34,052 --> 00:39:37,452
A. Nuchal skinfold thickening (bracket).

272
00:39:37,452 --> 00:39:41,327
B. Echogenic intracardiac focus (arrow).

273
00:39:41,327 --> 00:39:46,314
C. Mild renal pelvis dilatation (pyelectasis) (arrows).

274
00:39:46,314 --> 00:39:49,339
D. Echogenic bowel (arrow).

275
00:39:49,339 --> 00:39:56,364
E. Clinodactyly—hypoplasia of the 5th finger middle phalanx creates an inward curvature (arrow).

276
00:39:56,364 --> 00:39:59,064
F. “Sandal-gap” (arrow).

277
00:39:59,064 --> 00:40:06,052
If an isolated marker is identified and aneuploidy screening has not yet been performed, it should be offered.

278
00:40:06,052 --> 00:40:18,414
A benefit of cfDNA screening is that isolated aneuploidy markers no longer factor into the aneuploidy risk, because the negative predictive value of a normal cfDNA test is so high.

279
00:40:18,414 --> 00:40:25,764
Conversely, if a cfDNA result is abnormal, the absence of minor markers is not considered reassuring.

280
00:40:25,764 --> 00:40:28,227
First-trimester Markers.

281
00:40:28,227 --> 00:40:34,764
Detection of first-trimester findings associated with aneuploidy requires specialized imaging.

282
00:40:34,764 --> 00:40:39,652
NT evaluation is discussed earlier (p. 337).

283
00:40:39,652 --> 00:40:47,064
Other first-trimester markers are not routinely used in the United States but may be available at specialized centers.

284
00:40:47,064 --> 00:40:56,302
The Perinatal Quality Foundation’s Nuchal Translucency Quality Review Program offers an education program in first-trimester nasal bone assessment.

285
00:40:56,302 --> 00:41:07,264
The Fetal Medicine Foundation also provides online instruction and certification in first-trimester assessment of nasal bone, ductus venosus flow, and tricuspid flow.

286
00:41:07,264 --> 00:41:21,239
There are additional benefits of first-trimester ultrasound evaluation in women who elect aneuploidy screening, including accurate assessment of gestational age and early detection of multifetal gestation or fetal demise.

287
00:41:21,239 --> 00:41:33,664
As discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 249), standard first-trimester sonography may identify selected major anomalies associated with aneuploidy, such as cystic hygroma.

288
00:41:33,664 --> 00:41:36,152
Second-trimester Markers.

289
00:41:36,152 --> 00:41:44,664
If analyte-based aneuploidy screening has been performed, minor markers may be used to modify the risk using likelihood ratios .

290
00:41:44,664 --> 00:41:48,627
The risk increases with the number of markers identified.

291
00:41:48,627 --> 00:41:54,339
Risk modification should follow a protocol that specifies criteria for each marker .

292
00:41:54,339 --> 00:41:56,802
Table 17-8.

293
00:41:56,802 --> 00:42:15,127
Likelihood Ratios and False-Positive Rates for Isolated Second-Trimester Markers Used in Trisomy 21 Screening Protocols The nuchal skinfold is imaged in the transcerebellar view of the head and is measured from the outer edge of the skull to the outer border of the skin (see Fig. 17-3A).

294
00:42:15,127 --> 00:42:22,327
The skinfold is considered thickened if it measures ≥6 mm at 15 to 20 weeks .

295
00:42:22,327 --> 00:42:36,227
This finding is seen in approximately 1 per 200 pregnancies and confers more than tenfold risk for trisomy 21 (Bromley, 2002; Nyberg, 2001; Smith-Bindman, 2001).

296
00:42:36,227 --> 00:42:47,577
An echogenic intracardiac focus is a focal papillary muscle calcification that is neither a structural nor functional cardiac abnormality (see Fig. 17-3B).

297
00:42:47,577 --> 00:42:51,639
It is present in 4 to 7 percent of normal fetuses.

298
00:42:51,639 --> 00:42:55,877
The fetal trisomy 21 risk is approximately doubled.

299
00:42:55,877 --> 00:43:00,389
The finding is more commonly identified in Asian individuals .

300
00:43:00,389 --> 00:43:05,489
Fetuses with trisomy 13 may have bilateral echogenic foci .

301
00:43:05,489 --> 00:43:10,602
Mild renal pelvis dilatation is usually transient or physiologic .

302
00:43:10,602 --> 00:43:22,239
The renal pelves are measured anterior-to-posterior in a transverse image of the kidneys, with calipers placed at the inner borders of the fluid collection (see Fig. 17-3C).

303
00:43:22,239 --> 00:43:28,452
A measurement ≥4 mm is found in about 2 percent of fetuses and

304
00:43:28,612 --> 00:43:32,624
approximately doubles the risk for trisomy 21.

305
00:43:32,574 --> 00:43:44,887
The degree of pelvic dilatation beyond 4 mm correlates with the likelihood of an underlying renal abnormality, and third-trimester sonography is recommended at approximately 32 weeks.

306
00:43:44,887 --> 00:43:52,949
Echogenic fetal bowel is defined as bowel that is sonographically as bright as fetal bone (see Fig. 17-3D).

307
00:43:52,949 --> 00:44:03,899
It is identified in approximately 0.5 percent of pregnancies and often represents a small amount of swallowed blood, not infrequently in the setting of MSAFP elevation.

308
00:44:03,899 --> 00:44:08,812
The fetal trisomy 21 risk is increased approximately sixfold.

309
00:44:08,812 --> 00:44:18,574
Echogenic bowel is also associated with fetal cytomegalovirus infection and cystic fibrosis— representing inspissated meconium in the latter.

310
00:44:18,574 --> 00:44:25,562
Follow-up ultrasound evaluation is suggested at 32 weeks’ gestation to evaluate fetal growth .

311
00:44:25,562 --> 00:44:35,137
For the purpose of Down syndrome screening, the fetal femur and humerus are considered short if below the 2.5th percentile for gestational age .

312
00:44:35,137 --> 00:44:40,512
Thirdtrimester ultrasound evaluation is suggested to assess fetal growth.

313
00:44:40,512 --> 00:44:47,224
Fetuses with trisomy 21 and short femur are not more likely to be growth restricted, however .

314
00:44:47,224 --> 00:44:56,274
The fetal nasal bone is absent in 30 percent of second-trimester fetuses with trisomy 21 and hypoplastic in more than 50 percent .

315
00:44:56,274 --> 00:45:08,199
Absent nasal bone occurs in approximately 1 in 200 euploid fetuses (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020e; Viora, 2005).

316
00:45:08,199 --> 00:45:13,037
The finding represents delayed ossification rather than true absence.

317
00:45:13,037 --> 00:45:17,912
The nasal bone is measured between 15 and 22 weeks’ gestation.

318
00:45:17,912 --> 00:45:28,374
Hypoplasia may be defined as shorter than 2.5 mm, as >2 standard deviations below the mean, or based on a ratio to the biparietal diameter .

319
00:45:28,374 --> 00:45:39,387
The fetal profile and nasal bone are components of the detailed fetal anatomic survey, but they are not assessed as part of the standard examination and thus not used for routine screening .

320
00:45:39,387 --> 00:45:48,037
One or more choroid plexus cysts are seen in 1 to 2 percent of euploid pregnancies (Fig. 16-5, p. 312).

321
00:45:48,037 --> 00:45:52,787
The cyst is a benign, normal variant of no clinical consequence.

322
00:45:52,787 --> 00:45:59,124
If associated with structural abnormalities however, the trisomy 18 risk is increased .

323
00:45:59,124 --> 00:46:02,312
Carrier Screening For Genetic Disorders.

324
00:46:02,312 --> 00:46:11,424
The goal of genetic carrier screening is to provide individuals with meaningful information to guide pregnancy planning according to their personal values .

325
00:46:11,424 --> 00:46:27,787
There are three approaches, each of which is an acceptable strategy: ethnicity-based carrier screening, panethnic screening (performed regardless of ethnicity), and expanded carrier screening—which is a type of panethnic screening performed for a large number of conditions.

326
00:46:27,787 --> 00:46:32,099
All carrier screening is optional and should be an informed choice.

327
00:46:32,099 --> 00:46:46,174
Carrier screening is ideally performed prior to conception so that both partners can have the opportunity to consider the results and pursue options such as preimplantation genetic testing (p. 348) or donor gametes.

328
00:46:46,174 --> 00:46:51,249
If the screen detects a genetic condition, partner screening should be offered.

329
00:46:51,249 --> 00:46:55,574
No screening test will identify all carriers of a condition.

330
00:46:55,574 --> 00:47:04,262
Genetic counseling should include the residual risk for the condition if the partner’s screen is negative, as well as prenatal diagnosis options .

331
00:47:04,262 --> 00:47:10,762
Prenatal diagnosis is increasingly available if disease-causing mutation or mutations are known.

332
00:47:10,762 --> 00:47:28,099
The Genetic Testing Registry of the National Institutes of Health contains detailed information regarding more than 18,000 genetic conditions and 78,000 genetic tests (www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/).

333
00:47:28,099 --> 00:47:33,362
In the setting of consanguinity, additional genetic counseling is indicated.

334
00:47:33,362 --> 00:47:43,062
Ethnicity-based carrier screening is offered for selected autosomal recessive conditions that are found in greater frequency in specific racial or ethnic groups.

335
00:47:43,062 --> 00:47:54,062
The founder effect occurs when an otherwise rare gene is found with greater frequency in a certain population and can be traced back to a single family member or small group of ancestors.

336
00:47:54,062 --> 00:48:03,749
It may develop when generations of individuals procreate only within their own groups because of religious or ethnic prohibitions or geographical isolation.

337
00:48:03,749 --> 00:48:07,112
Because assignment of a single ethnicity is

338
00:48:07,276 --> 00:48:11,851
often difficult, panethnic screening is increasingly preferred.

339
00:48:11,801 --> 00:48:20,663
Expanded carrier screening has the advantage of screening for many conditions simultaneously, ranging from a few dozen to more than 1000 .

340
00:48:20,663 --> 00:48:30,188
An unfortunate result is that much of the population— more than 50 percent of those screened–may be identified to be carriers for at least 1 condition.

341
00:48:30,188 --> 00:48:36,138
Some conditions are so rare that information about detection and residual risk are limited.

342
00:48:36,138 --> 00:48:45,901
Counseling regarding phenotypic expression of different mutations and determination of whether variants have clinical significance has become increasingly complex.

343
00:48:45,901 --> 00:48:55,001
Thus, expanded carrier screening can cause anxiety for families and may pose challenges to already-strained genetic counseling resources.

344
00:48:55,001 --> 00:49:13,951
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) has the following guidelines for conditions included in expanded panels: 1. The carrier frequency should be at least 1:100, which corresponds to a population prevalence of 1:40,000.

345
00:49:13,951 --> 00:49:26,013
2. The condition should have a well-defined phenotype, lead to a detrimental effect on quality of life, cause cognitive or physical impairment, or require surgical or medical intervention.

346
00:49:26,013 --> 00:49:30,851
3. Conditions with adult onset are not recommended for inclusion.

347
00:49:30,851 --> 00:49:38,576
There is a long-held principle that genetic carrier screening for a particular condition not be repeated in a subsequent pregnancy.

348
00:49:38,576 --> 00:49:47,363
The number of mutations for which testing is available has greatly increased in recent years, but the additional yield of rescreening is usually low.

349
00:49:47,363 --> 00:49:53,538
Consultation with a provider with genetic expertise should be considered before screening is repeated .

350
00:49:53,538 --> 00:49:56,301
# Cystic Fibrosis.

351
00:49:56,301 --> 00:50:09,501
This disorder is caused by a mutation in the cystic fibrosis conductance transmembrane regulator (CFTR) gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 7 and encodes a chloride-channel protein.

352
00:50:09,501 --> 00:50:22,001
Although the most common CFTR mutation associated with classic cystic fibrosis (CF) is the ΔF508 mutation, more than 2100 mutations have been identified .

353
00:50:22,001 --> 00:50:29,476
CF may develop from either homozygosity or compound heterozygosity for mutations in the CFTR gene.

354
00:50:29,476 --> 00:50:35,626
One mutation must be present in each copy of the gene, but they need not be the same mutation.

355
00:50:35,626 --> 00:50:39,201
There is a wide range of clinical disease severity.

356
00:50:39,201 --> 00:50:47,913
Median survival length is 42 years, but approximately 15 percent have milder disease and can survive for decades longer.

357
00:50:47,913 --> 00:50:54,201
Care of the pregnant woman with CF is discussed in Chapter 54 (p. 968).

358
00:50:54,201 --> 00:51:07,163
For more than two decades, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) has recommended that all women who are pregnant or considering pregnancy be offered CF carrier screening.

359
00:51:07,163 --> 00:51:18,776
Such screening should include, at minimum, a panel of 23 panethnic gene mutations, selected because they are present in at least 0.1 percent of patients with classic CF.

360
00:51:18,776 --> 00:51:35,401
The CF-mutation carrier frequency approximates 1:25 in Ashkenazi Jews and those of non-Hispanic white ethnicity, 1:60 in African Americans and those of Hispanic white ethnicity, and 1:95 Asian Americans.

361
00:51:35,401 --> 00:51:49,401
Using the 23mutation panel, the residual carrier risk after a negative test result was roughly 1:380 for Ashkenazi Jews and 1:170 to 1:200 for other ethnicities .

362
00:51:49,401 --> 00:52:00,438
More recently however, expanded panels that use gene sequencing have been reported to improve detection of at-risk couples by 30 percent compared with the 23-mutation panel .

363
00:52:00,438 --> 00:52:14,976
The American College of Medical Genetics now recommends either a targeted approach to carrier screening, in which the standard mutation panel may be used, or a more comprehensive approach in which specific variant classifications may be reported .

364
00:52:14,976 --> 00:52:23,913
CF is included in all newborn screening panels but is not a substitute for carrier screening because it only identifies affected individuals.

365
00:52:23,913 --> 00:52:28,813
If the patient carries a mutation, her partner should be offered screening.

366
00:52:28,813 --> 00:52:36,326
If both parents are carriers, genetic counseling is indicated, and prenatal diagnostic testing should be offered.

367
00:52:36,326 --> 00:52:39,413
# Spinal Muscular Atrophy.

368
00:52:39,413 --> 00:52:47,601
This autosomal recessive disorder results in spinal cord motor neuron degeneration that leads to skeletal muscle atrophy and

369
00:52:47,764 --> 00:52:49,964
generalized weakness.

370
00:52:49,914 --> 00:52:52,926
There is currently no effective treatment.

371
00:52:52,926 --> 00:53:00,289
The prevalence of spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is 1 in 6000 to 10,000 live births.

372
00:53:00,289 --> 00:53:15,501
Types I, II, III, and IV are caused by mutations in the survival motor neuron (SMN1) gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q13.2) and encodes the SMN protein.

373
00:53:15,501 --> 00:53:20,351
Types I and II account for 80 percent of cases and are both lethal .

374
00:53:20,351 --> 00:53:25,514
SMA type I, known as Werdnig-Hoffmann disease, is the most severe.

375
00:53:25,514 --> 00:53:33,076
Disease onset is within the first 6 months, and affected children die of respiratory failure by age 2 years.

376
00:53:33,076 --> 00:53:41,014
Type II generally has onset before age 2 years, and the age at death can range from 2 years to the third decade of life.

377
00:53:41,014 --> 00:53:47,926
Type III also presents before age 2 years, with disease severity that is milder and more variable.

378
00:53:47,926 --> 00:53:51,226
Type IV does not present until adulthood.

379
00:53:51,226 --> 00:54:03,839
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends that carrier screening for SMA be offered to all women who are considering pregnancy or are currently pregnant.

380
00:54:03,839 --> 00:54:11,176
Prior to screening, the potential spectrum of severity, carrier frequency, and detection rate should be provided.

381
00:54:11,176 --> 00:54:21,664
Posttest counseling should include the residual risk after a negative result, which differs according to ethnicity and also according to the number of SMN1 copies detected.

382
00:54:21,664 --> 00:54:40,151
The SMA carrier frequency approximates 1:35 in those of non-Hispanic white ethnicity, 1:41 in Ashkenazi Jews, 1:53 in Asians, 1:66 in African Americans, and 1:117 in those of Hispanic white ethnicity .

383
00:54:40,151 --> 00:54:49,501
Carrier detection rates range from 90 to 95 percent for each ethnicity except African Americans, in whom it approximates 70 percent .

384
00:54:49,501 --> 00:55:01,526
Although there is usually one copy of the SMN1 gene on each chromosome, approximately 3 to 4 percent of carriers have no copies of this gene on one chromosome and two copies on the other.

385
00:55:01,526 --> 00:55:09,701
Because such individuals have two copies in total, they are not detected if a quantitative polymerase chain reaction test is used.

386
00:55:09,701 --> 00:55:17,776
Carrier screening is less sensitive in African American individuals, because they are more likely to have this genetic variation.

387
00:55:17,776 --> 00:55:23,589
Additionally, some individuals have three copies of the gene and are at even lower risk.

388
00:55:23,589 --> 00:55:32,226
If the patient or her partner has a family history of SMA, or if carrier screening is positive, genetic counseling is indicated.

389
00:55:32,226 --> 00:55:35,076
# Hemoglobinopathies.

390
00:55:35,076 --> 00:55:45,439
These include the sickle hemoglobinopathies—sickle cell anemia and sickle cell hemoglobin C disease, thalassemias, and sickle cell–β-thalassemia.

391
00:55:45,439 --> 00:55:52,051
Their pathophysiology and management are discussed in Chapter 59 (p. 1053).

392
00:55:52,051 --> 00:56:00,714
The risk is increased in women who are of African or AfricanAmerican, Mediterranean, Middle Eastern, or Southeast Asian ethnicity.

393
00:56:00,714 --> 00:56:07,626
In such individuals, hemoglobin electrophoresis should be offered prenatally or prior to conception .

394
00:56:07,626 --> 00:56:19,601
Sickle Hemoglobinopathies Approximately 1 in 12 African Americans has sickle-cell trait, 1 in 40 carries hemoglobin C, and 1 in 40 carries the trait for β-thalassemia.

395
00:56:19,601 --> 00:56:27,239
Hemoglobin S is also more common among individuals of Mediterranean, Middle Eastern, and Asian Indian descent .

396
00:56:27,239 --> 00:56:34,026
If a couple is at risk to have a child with a sickle hemoglobinopathy, genetic counseling should be offered.

397
00:56:34,026 --> 00:56:40,389
Prenatal diagnosis can be performed with either chorionic villus sampling or amniocentesis.

398
00:56:40,389 --> 00:56:45,214
Thalassemias These are the most common single-gene disorders worldwide.

399
00:56:45,214 --> 00:56:54,464
Thalassemias are characterized as alpha (α) or beta (β) depending on whether α- or βhemoglobin chains are affected.

400
00:56:54,464 --> 00:57:05,764
In general, α-thalassemia is more likely to be caused by deletions of α-globin chains, whereas β-thalassemia more often stems from mutations in β-globin chains.

401
00:57:05,764 --> 00:57:12,551
In α-thalassemia, the number of α-globin genes that are deleted may range from one to all four.

402
00:57:12,551 --> 00:57:26,226
If two α-globin genes are deleted, both may be deleted from the same chromosome—cis configuration (αα/––), or one may be deleted from each chromosome—trans configuration (α–/α–).

403
00:57:26,226 --> 00:57:35,626
The cis configuration is more prevalent among Southeast Asians, whereas those of African descent are more likely to inherit the trans configuration.

404
00:57:35,626 --> 00:57:37,676
Alphathalassemia

405
00:57:37,828 --> 00:57:40,503
trait results in mild anemia.

406
00:57:40,453 --> 00:57:50,015
However, if the patient and her partner both carry cis deletions, offspring are at risk for an absence of αhemoglobin, called Hb Barts disease.

407
00:57:50,015 --> 00:57:53,315
This can lead to hydrops and fetal loss .

408
00:57:53,315 --> 00:57:59,453
Hemoglobin electrophoresis cannot detect α-thalassemia or α-thalassemia trait.

409
00:57:59,453 --> 00:58:12,590
Molecular genetic testing should be considered if there is microcytic anemia in the absence of iron deficiency and if the hemoglobin electrophoresis is normal, particularly among individuals of Southeast Asian descent .

410
00:58:12,590 --> 00:58:19,678
In β-thalassemia, β-globin genes may cause reduced or absent production of β-globin chains.

411
00:58:19,678 --> 00:58:24,890
If the mutation affects one gene, it results in β-thalassemia minor.

412
00:58:24,890 --> 00:58:33,603
If both copies are affected, the result is either β-thalassemia major— termed Cooley anemia—or β-thalassemia intermedia.

413
00:58:33,603 --> 00:58:42,728
Hemoglobin electrophoresis demonstrates elevated levels of hemoglobins that do not contain β-chains, which are hemoglobins F and A2.

414
00:58:42,728 --> 00:58:53,053
β-Thalassemia minor should be considered if an individual of one or more of the aforementioned ethnicities is found to have microcytic anemia in the absence of iron deficiency.

415
00:58:53,053 --> 00:58:58,915
A hemoglobin A2 level exceeding 3.5 percent confirms the diagnosis.

416
00:58:58,915 --> 00:59:03,615
# Recessive Diseases in Ashkenazi Jewish Individuals.

417
00:59:03,615 --> 00:59:20,390
Among Jewish individuals of Eastern and Central European (Ashkenazi) descent, there are three severe diseases for which the carrier frequency and detection rate are high enough that screening should be offered: Tay-Sachs disease, Canavan disease, and familial dysautonomia .

418
00:59:20,390 --> 00:59:30,140
The carrier rate approximates 1 in 30 for Tay-Sachs disease, 1 in 40 for Canavan disease, and 1 in 32 for familial dysautonomia.

419
00:59:30,140 --> 00:59:34,328
For each, the detection rate is at least 98 percent.

420
00:59:34,328 --> 00:59:41,578
As for other genetic conditions, screening is ideally performed prior to conception or during early pregnancy.

421
00:59:41,578 --> 00:59:48,653
There are several other autosomal recessive conditions for which the College also recommends that screening be considered.

422
00:59:48,653 --> 01:00:06,565
These include Bloom syndrome, familial hyperinsulinism, Fanconi anemia, Gaucher disease, glycogen storage disease type I (von Gierke disease), Joubert syndrome, maple syrup urine disease, mucolipidosis type IV, Niemann-Pick disease, and Usher syndrome.

423
01:00:06,565 --> 01:00:17,403
Gaucher disease differs from the other conditions listed because it has a wide range in phenotype and because treatment is available in the form of enzyme repletion and substrate reduction therapy.

424
01:00:17,403 --> 01:00:24,878
Tay-Sachs Disease This lysosomal-storage disease causes progressive neurodegeneration and death in early childhood.

425
01:00:24,878 --> 01:00:34,790
It is characterized by deficiency of the enzyme hexosaminidase A, which results in accumulation of GM2 gangliosides in the central nervous system.

426
01:00:34,790 --> 01:00:45,078
Affected individuals have almost complete absence of the enzyme, whereas carriers are asymptomatic but have less than 55percent hexosaminidase A activity.

427
01:00:45,078 --> 01:00:55,265
An international Tay-Sachs carrier screening campaign was initiated in the 1970s and met with unprecedented success in the Ashkenazi Jewish population.

428
01:00:55,265 --> 01:01:00,328
The incidence of Tay-Sachs disease subsequently declined more than 90 percent .

429
01:01:00,328 --> 01:01:06,678
Other groups at increased risk for Tay-Sachs disease include those of FrenchCanadian and Cajun descent.

430
01:01:06,678 --> 01:01:14,715
Carrier screening for Tay-Sachs disease is performed with DNA-based mutation analysis or hexosaminidase activity testing.

431
01:01:14,715 --> 01:01:25,103
DNA-based mutation analysis is the preferred test in Ashkenazi Jewish individuals and other highrisk groups but has a lower detection rate in the general population.

432
01:01:25,103 --> 01:01:32,953
Therefore, hexosaminidase activity testing is recommended for screening in individuals from lower-risk ethnicities.

433
01:01:32,953 --> 01:01:42,715
If a woman is pregnant or taking oral contraceptives, a hexosaminidase activity test should be performed on leukocytes to avoid a high false-positive rate .

434
01:01:42,715 --> 01:01:48,603
If both partners are found to be carriers, prenatal diagnostic testing should be offered.

435
01:01:48,603 --> 01:01:54,390
Hexosaminidase activity may be measured from chorionic villi or amnionic fluid.

436
01:01:54,390 --> 01:01:57,915
Prenatal And Preimplantation Diagnostic.

437
01:01:57,915 --> 01:01:59,440
Tests.

438
01:01:59,440 --> 01:02:07,615
Diagnostic procedures used in prenatal diagnosis include amniocentesis and chorionic villus sampling (CVS).

439
01:02:07,615 --> 01:02:11,978
Fetal blood sampling is rarely performed solely for genetic

440
01:02:12,148 --> 01:02:17,123
diagnosis but may be used in conjunction with intrauterine transfusion.

441
01:02:17,073 --> 01:02:28,835
Preimplantation genetic tests for aneuploidies, structural chromosomal rearrangements, and monogenic disorders are available for couples undergoing in vitro fertilization (IVF).

442
01:02:28,835 --> 01:02:36,560
In the setting of a fetal structural abnormality, chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA) is recommended.

443
01:02:36,560 --> 01:02:55,785
CMA is preferred to cytogenetic analysis (karyotyping) because it can detect clinically significant chromosomal abnormalities in approximately 6.5 percent of fetuses with normal karyotype (Callaway, 2013; de Wit, 2014, Wapner, 2012).

444
01:02:55,785 --> 01:03:09,085
And, in the absence of a fetal structural abnormality, CMA has detected additional chromosomal abnormalities (pathogenic copy number variants) in up to 1 percent of those with normal karyotype.

445
01:03:09,085 --> 01:03:21,723
CMA is therefore made available whenever a prenatal diagnostic procedure is performed (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Callaway, 2013).

446
01:03:21,723 --> 01:03:29,698
Types of CMA platforms and their benefits and limitations are reviewed in Chapter 16 (p. 326).

447
01:03:29,698 --> 01:03:33,823
Karyotyping identifies aneuploidy and polyploidy.

448
01:03:33,823 --> 01:03:40,960
Additionally, it can identify balanced chromosomal rearrangements that are currently undetectable with CMA.

449
01:03:40,960 --> 01:03:51,360
For example, karyotyping performed when a couple has experienced recurrent pregnancy loss may reveal that one parent carries a balanced robertsonian translocation .

450
01:03:51,360 --> 01:04:05,110
An additional indication is the fetus with a structural abnormality that strongly suggests a particular aneuploidy—such as endocardial cushion defect in trisomy 21 or holoprosencephaly in trisomy 13.

451
01:04:05,110 --> 01:04:20,523
If rapid identification of a specific chromosomal abnormality, such as trisomy 21, 18, or 13, is needed, fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) may be used in conjunction with CMA or karyotyping.

452
01:04:20,523 --> 01:04:30,223
Decision-making based on FISH should incorporate clinical information consistent with the suspected diagnosis, such as a screening result or ultrasound finding .

453
01:04:30,223 --> 01:04:38,423
Because of improvements in aneuploidy screening tests, there has been a dramatic drop in the number of prenatal diagnostic procedures.

454
01:04:38,423 --> 01:04:52,123
Larion and coworkers (2014) reported a 70-percent decline in CVS and a nearly 50percent drop in amniocentesis procedures since the introduction of cfDNA screening in 2012.

455
01:04:52,123 --> 01:04:59,523
This further amplified the decrease in amniocentesis procedures that occurred with the advent of first-trimester screening .

456
01:04:59,523 --> 01:05:06,773
Unfortunately, fewer than 40 percent of women with a positive screening result elect prenatal diagnosis .

457
01:05:06,773 --> 01:05:14,048
If prenatal diagnosis is elected, however, the percent with an abnormal result has increased significantly .

458
01:05:14,048 --> 01:05:16,735
# Amniocentesis.

459
01:05:16,735 --> 01:05:23,223
Because of its established safety and efficacy, amniocentesis is offered to all pregnant women .

460
01:05:23,223 --> 01:05:28,798
Amnionic fluid is withdrawn transabdominally under direct ultrasound guidance.

461
01:05:28,798 --> 01:05:37,310
Although typically performed between 15 and 20 weeks’ gestation, amniocentesis may be done at any point later in gestation.

462
01:05:37,310 --> 01:05:43,598
Tests include those for genetic conditions, congenital infections, and alloimmunization.

463
01:05:43,598 --> 01:05:51,648
Fetal lung maturity is no longer routinely assessed, based on recommendations against using it to assist with delivery timing .

464
01:05:51,648 --> 01:05:58,410
Technique Amniocentesis is performed with a 20- or 22-gauge needle using aseptic technique .

465
01:05:58,410 --> 01:06:02,235
A standard spinal needle is 9 cm long.

466
01:06:02,235 --> 01:06:06,010
A longer needle may be required for obese patients.

467
01:06:06,010 --> 01:06:14,523
To aid in needle selection, we find it helpful to measure the distance from skin to amnionic fluid with ultrasound prior to the procedure.

468
01:06:14,523 --> 01:06:21,110
After the patient empties her bladder, the skin is prepared with an antiseptic such as povidone-iodine.

469
01:06:21,110 --> 01:06:26,560
Shellfish allergy is not a contraindication to the povidone-iodine antiseptic .

470
01:06:26,560 --> 01:06:29,098
Figure 17-4.

471
01:06:29,098 --> 01:06:31,573
A. Amniocentesis.

472
01:06:31,573 --> 01:06:37,148
B. The amniocentesis needle is seen in the upper right portion of this sonogram.

473
01:06:37,148 --> 01:06:49,373
Figures 17-4, 17-5, and 17-7 are reproduced with permission from Mastrobattista JM, Espinoza J: Invasive prenatal diagnostic procedures.

474
01:06:49,373 --> 01:06:58,685
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

475
01:06:58,685 --> 01:07:03,173
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

476
01:07:03,173 --> 01:07:08,335
All aspects of the procedure are performed under direct ultrasound guidance.

477
01:07:08,335 --> 01:07:09,635
A

478
01:07:09,796 --> 01:07:17,946
pocket of amnionic fluid close to the midline is selected, avoiding fetal parts and being cognizant of uterine size and shape.

479
01:07:17,896 --> 01:07:26,671
The needle is inserted perpendicular to the skin, gently puncturing the chorioamnion rather than pushing or “tenting” it away from the uterine wall.

480
01:07:26,671 --> 01:07:36,621
The amnion usually fuses with the underlying chorion by 16 weeks’ gestation, and the procedure is generally deferred until after chorioamnion fusion.

481
01:07:36,621 --> 01:07:44,858
Discomfort from the procedure is considered minor and is related more to focal myometrial contraction than needle discomfort at the skin.

482
01:07:44,858 --> 01:07:48,708
Local anesthetic has not been found to be beneficial .

483
01:07:48,708 --> 01:07:53,208
The amnionic fluid should be clear and colorless or pale yellow.

484
01:07:53,208 --> 01:08:00,683
Bloodtinged fluid is more frequent with transplacental passage of the needle and generally clears with continued aspiration.

485
01:08:00,683 --> 01:08:14,296
Needle passage through the placenta occurs in approximately 60 percent of cases with anterior placentation and is not associated with pregnancy loss (Bombard, 1995, Marthin, 1997).

486
01:08:14,296 --> 01:08:19,971
Dark brown or greenish fluid may represent a past episode of intraamnionic bleeding.

487
01:08:19,971 --> 01:08:26,696
The volume of fluid generally needed for commonly performed analyses is shown in Table 17-9.

488
01:08:26,696 --> 01:08:34,646
Because the initial 1 to 2 mL of fluid aspirate may be contaminated with maternal cells, it is often discarded.

489
01:08:34,646 --> 01:08:43,021
Approximately 20 to 30 mL of fluid is then collected for either fetal CMA or karyotyping before the needle is withdrawn.

490
01:08:43,021 --> 01:08:50,271
The uterine puncture site is observed for bleeding, and fetal cardiac motion is documented following the procedure.

491
01:08:50,271 --> 01:08:58,071
If the patient is Rh D-negative and unsensitized, anti-D immune globulin is administered after the procedure .

492
01:08:58,071 --> 01:09:00,621
Table 17-9.

493
01:09:00,621 --> 01:09:07,308
Selected Tests Performed on Amnionic Fluid and Volume of Fluid Required Multifetal Pregnancy.

494
01:09:07,308 --> 01:09:18,033
When performing the procedure in a dichorionic or monochorionic diamnionic twin gestation, careful attention is paid to the location of each sac and the dividing membrane.

495
01:09:18,033 --> 01:09:32,171
Until recently, a small quantity of dilute indigo carmine dye was often injected before removing the needle from the first sac, with return of clear amnionic fluid anticipated following needle placement into the second sac.

496
01:09:32,171 --> 01:09:41,083
Because of widespread shortages of indigo carmine dye, experienced providers typically offer amniocentesis without dye injection.

497
01:09:41,083 --> 01:09:51,383
Methylene blue dye is contraindicated because it has been associated with jejunal atresia and neonatal methemoglobinemia (van der Pol, 1992).

498
01:09:51,383 --> 01:09:59,171
Complications Midtrimester losses attributable to amniocentesis have decreased with improvements in imaging technology.

499
01:09:59,171 --> 01:10:19,033
When performed by an experienced provider, the procedure-related loss rate is estimated to be 0.1 to 0.3 percent, or about 1 per 500 procedures (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Odibo, 2008, Salomon, 2019).

500
01:10:19,033 --> 01:10:25,508
The loss rate may be doubled in women whose body mass index exceeds 40 kg/m2 .

501
01:10:25,508 --> 01:10:34,871
In twin pregnancies, Cahill and coworkers (2009) reported a loss rate of 1.8 percent attributable to amniocentesis.

502
01:10:34,871 --> 01:10:44,283
When counseling regarding fetal loss following a procedure, it can be helpful to explain the difference between overall losses and procedurerelated losses.

503
01:10:44,283 --> 01:11:00,796
In a recent metaanalysis that included more than 60,000 amniocentesis procedures and 300,000 control pregnancies, the loss rate in the absence of a procedure was 0.6 percent, approximately twice the rate of losses attributable to procedures .

504
01:11:00,796 --> 01:11:11,258
The indication for the procedure is also relevant, because severe fetal abnormalities and hydrops can significantly increase the loss rate, regardless of a procedure.

505
01:11:11,258 --> 01:11:19,346
Other complications of amniocentesis include amnionic fluid leakage or transient vaginal spotting in 1 to 2 percent.

506
01:11:19,346 --> 01:11:24,896
Leakage of amnionic fluid generally occurs within 48 hours of the procedure.

507
01:11:24,896 --> 01:11:31,608
Leakage of fluid typically resolves after a few days, and fetal survival exceeds 90 percent .

508
01:11:31,608 --> 01:11:37,246
Management recommendations include pelvic rest and avoidance of strenuous activity.

509
01:11:37,246 --> 01:11:40,296
Needle injuries to the fetus are rare.

510
01:11:40,296 --> 01:11:50,596
If karyotyping is performed, amnionic fluid culture is successful in more than 99 percent of cases, although cells are less likely to grow if the fetus is

511
01:11:50,764 --> 01:11:52,401
abnormal .

512
01:11:52,351 --> 01:11:59,301
Early Amniocentesis Amniocentesis was formerly offered between 11 and 14 weeks’ gestation.

513
01:11:59,301 --> 01:12:08,614
The technique is the same as for traditional amniocentesis, but the procedure may be more challenging because of lack of membrane fusion to the uterine wall.

514
01:12:08,614 --> 01:12:13,314
Approximately 1 mL is withdrawn for each week of gestation.

515
01:12:13,314 --> 01:12:22,426
Early amniocentesis is associated with significantly higher rates of fetal loss and other procedure-related complications than other procedures.

516
01:12:22,426 --> 01:12:36,026
Complications include development of talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) and amnionic fluid leakage (Canadian Early and Mid-Trimester Amniocentesis Trial, 1998; Philip, 2004).

517
01:12:36,026 --> 01:12:40,551
For these reasons, early amniocentesis is not recommended .

518
01:12:40,551 --> 01:12:43,639
# Chorionic Villus Sampling.

519
01:12:43,639 --> 01:12:49,839
Biopsy of chorionic villi is typically performed between 10 and 13 weeks’ gestation.

520
01:12:49,839 --> 01:12:56,601
As with amniocentesis, the specimen is usually sent for CMA or karyotype analysis.

521
01:12:56,601 --> 01:13:07,126
The primary advantage of CVS is that results are available earlier in pregnancy, permitting more time for decision-making and safer pregnancy termination, if desired.

522
01:13:07,126 --> 01:13:11,939
Technique CVS is performed transcervically or transabdominally.

523
01:13:11,939 --> 01:13:15,389
Both approaches are equally safe and effective .

524
01:13:15,389 --> 01:13:22,726
The transcervical approach uses a flexible polyethylene catheter that contains a blunt-tipped, malleable stylet.

525
01:13:22,726 --> 01:13:28,351
Transabdominal sampling is performed with an 18- or 20-gauge spinal needle.

526
01:13:28,351 --> 01:13:34,314
Both use aseptic technique and are performed under direct transabdominal ultrasound guidance.

527
01:13:34,314 --> 01:13:44,189
The catheter or needle is inserted into the early placenta—chorion frondosum, and villi are aspirated into a syringe that contains tissue culture media .

528
01:13:44,189 --> 01:13:46,801
Figure 17-5.

529
01:13:46,801 --> 01:13:50,314
A. Transcervical chorionic villus sampling.

530
01:13:50,314 --> 01:13:54,314
B. Catheter entering the placenta is marked and labeled.

531
01:13:54,314 --> 01:14:06,651
Relative contraindications include vaginal bleeding or spotting, active genital tract infection, extreme uterine ante- or retroflexion, or body habitus precluding adequate visualization.

532
01:14:06,651 --> 01:14:14,451
If the patient is Rh D-negative and unsensitized, anti-D immune globulin is administered after the procedure.

533
01:14:14,451 --> 01:14:21,939
Complications The overall fetal loss rate following CVS is higher than that with midtrimester amniocentesis.

534
01:14:21,939 --> 01:14:28,501
This is because of losses that would have occurred between the first and second trimester in the absence of a procedure.

535
01:14:28,501 --> 01:14:36,964
The procedure-related loss rate is comparable to that with amniocentesis, approximately 0.1 to 0.3 percent .

536
01:14:36,964 --> 01:14:45,714
In fact, a recent metaanalysis identified no significant losses from CVS when controls with similar risk profiles were selected .

537
01:14:45,714 --> 01:14:50,814
As with amniocentesis, CVS indication will affect the loss rate.

538
01:14:50,814 --> 01:14:56,814
For example, fetuses with increased NT thickness have a greater likelihood of demise.

539
01:14:56,814 --> 01:15:05,426
There is also a learning curve associated with safe performance of CVS (Silver, 1990; Wijnberger, 2003).

540
01:15:05,426 --> 01:15:20,489
An early problem with CVS was its association with limb-reduction defects and oromandibular limb hypogenesis, shown in Figure 17-6 (Firth, 1991, 1994; Hsieh, 1995).

541
01:15:20,489 --> 01:15:26,789
These were subsequently found to be associated with procedures performed at 7 weeks’ gestation .

542
01:15:26,789 --> 01:15:41,051
When performed at or after 10 weeks’ gestation, the incidence of limb defects does not exceed the background population rate of approximately 1 case per 1000 (Evans, 2005; Kuliev, 1996).

543
01:15:41,051 --> 01:15:43,651
Figure 17-6.

544
01:15:43,651 --> 01:15:52,314
Oromandibular limb hypogenesis is characterized by transverse limb deficiency and absence or hypoplasia of the tongue or mandible.

545
01:15:52,314 --> 01:15:57,901
This is hypothesized to result from vascular disruption with subsequent loss of tissue.

546
01:15:57,901 --> 01:16:05,501
A. Sonogram obtained at 25 weeks’ gestation demonstrates a fetal limb reduction defect involving the right hand.

547
01:16:05,501 --> 01:16:09,564
B. Photograph of the right extremity of the same newborn.

548
01:16:09,564 --> 01:16:13,889
Chorionic villus sampling was not performed in this pregnancy.

549
01:16:13,889 --> 01:16:17,626
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Jamie Morgan.

550
01:16:17,626 --> 01:16:26,101
Vaginal spotting is not uncommon following transcervical sampling, but it is self-limited and not associated with pregnancy loss.

551
01:16:26,101 --> 01:16:32,264
The incidence of infection or leakage of amnionic fluid is less than 0.5 percent .

552
01:16:32,264 --> 01:16:39,076
A limitation of CVS is that chromosomal mosaicism is identified in up to 2 percent of specimens .

553
01:16:39,076 --> 01:16:43,776
In most cases, the mosaicism reflects confined placental

554
01:16:43,948 --> 01:16:48,360
mosaicism rather than a true second cell line within the fetus.

555
01:16:48,310 --> 01:16:52,710
This is discussed in Chapter 16 (p. 318).

556
01:16:52,710 --> 01:17:00,848
Amniocentesis should be offered, and if the result is normal, the mosaicism is usually presumed to be confined to the placenta.

557
01:17:00,848 --> 01:17:09,935
Confined placental mosaicism is associated with fetal-growth restriction (Baffero, 2012; Toutain, 2018).

558
01:17:09,935 --> 01:17:12,723
# Fetal Blood Sampling.

559
01:17:12,723 --> 01:17:18,710
This procedure is also called cordocentesis or percutaneous umbilical blood sampling .

560
01:17:18,710 --> 01:17:29,698
It was initially described for fetal transfusion of red blood cells in the setting of anemia from alloimmunization, and fetal anemia assessment remains the most common indication .

561
01:17:29,698 --> 01:17:42,785
Other indications include assessment and treatment of platelet alloimmunization and for fetal karyotype determination, particularly in cases of mosaicism identified following amniocentesis or CVS.

562
01:17:42,785 --> 01:17:50,660
Improvements in testing using an amniocentesis specimen have eliminated the need for fetal blood sampling in most cases .

563
01:17:50,660 --> 01:17:53,235
Figure 17-7.

564
01:17:53,235 --> 01:17:55,460
Fetal blood sampling.

565
01:17:55,460 --> 01:18:01,510
A. Access to the umbilical vein varies depending on placental location and cord position.

566
01:18:01,510 --> 01:18:06,160
With an anterior placenta, the needle may traverse the placenta.

567
01:18:06,160 --> 01:18:13,998
Inset: With posterior placentation, the needle passes through amnionic fluid before penetrating the umbilical vein.

568
01:18:13,998 --> 01:18:18,010
Alternatively, a free loop of cord may be accessed.

569
01:18:18,010 --> 01:18:25,723
B. Sonogram shows an anterior placenta with transplacental needle passage into the umbilical vein (UV).

570
01:18:25,723 --> 01:18:38,885
Technique Using aseptic technique, a 22- or 23-gauge spinal needle is introduced into the umbilical vein under direct ultrasound guidance, and blood is slowly withdrawn into a heparinized syringe.

571
01:18:38,885 --> 01:18:42,323
Precise visualization of the needle is essential.

572
01:18:42,323 --> 01:18:47,823
As with amniocentesis, a longer needle may be required for obese patients.

573
01:18:47,823 --> 01:18:57,173
Fetal blood sampling is often performed near the placental cord insertion site, where it may be easier to enter the cord if the placenta lies anteriorly.

574
01:18:57,173 --> 01:19:01,135
Alternatively, a free loop of cord may be punctured.

575
01:19:01,135 --> 01:19:08,798
Because fetal blood sampling requires more time than other fetal procedures, a local anesthetic may be administered.

576
01:19:08,798 --> 01:19:15,273
Prophylactic antibiotics are used at some centers, although no trials support this policy.

577
01:19:15,273 --> 01:19:21,485
Arterial puncture is avoided, because it may result in vasospasm and fetal bradycardia.

578
01:19:21,485 --> 01:19:28,260
After the needle is removed, fetal cardiac motion is documented, and the site is observed for bleeding.

579
01:19:28,260 --> 01:19:39,235
Complications The procedure-related fetal loss rate following fetal blood sampling approximates 1 percent (Tongsong, 2001, Tanvisut, 2020).

580
01:19:39,235 --> 01:19:44,885
The actual loss rate varies according to the procedure indication and the fetal status.

581
01:19:44,885 --> 01:20:06,923
Other complications may include cord vessel bleeding in 20 to 30 percent of cases, fetal-maternal bleeding in approximately 40 percent of cases in which the placenta is traversed, and fetal bradycardia in 5 to 10 percent (Boupaijit, 2012; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2013; Tanvisut, 2020).

582
01:20:06,923 --> 01:20:13,248
Most complications are transitory, with complete recovery, but some result in fetal loss.

583
01:20:13,248 --> 01:20:29,160
In one series of more than 2000 procedures comparing fetal blood sampling near the placental cord insertion site with puncture of a free loop, rates of procedure success, pregnancy loss, visible bleeding from the cord, and fetal bradycardia did not differ.

584
01:20:29,160 --> 01:20:38,723
Time to complete the procedure was significantly shorter if the cord was sampled at the placental insertion site rather than in a free loop—5 versus 7 minutes.

585
01:20:38,723 --> 01:20:44,610
However, sampling at the insertion site had a higher rate of maternal blood contamination .

586
01:20:44,610 --> 01:20:48,085
# Preimplantation Genetic Tests.

587
01:20:48,085 --> 01:20:58,123
For couples undergoing IVF, several types of genetic tests may be performed on oocytes (polar bodies) or embryos prior to uterine transfer.

588
01:20:58,123 --> 01:21:13,685
These include preimplantation genetic testing for aneuploidy, abbreviated PGT-A, prenatal genetic testing for structural rearrangements (PGT-SR), and prenatal genetic testing for monogenic disorders (PGT-M).

589
01:21:13,685 --> 01:21:25,023
Testing is typically performed via trophectoderm biopsy at the blastocyst stage but may be performed on polar bodies of oocytes or via blastomere biopsy of a cleavage-stage embryo .

590
01:21:25,023 --> 01:21:29,135
False-positive and false-negative results are possible with

591
01:21:29,284 --> 01:21:31,759
each type of PGT.

592
01:21:31,709 --> 01:21:37,621
Therefore, prenatal diagnosis with CVS or amniocentesis should be offered .

593
01:21:37,621 --> 01:21:43,646
Comprehensive genetic counseling is required before consideration of each of these procedures .

594
01:21:43,646 --> 01:21:46,109
Figure 17-8.

595
01:21:46,109 --> 01:21:48,646
A. Blastomere biopsy.

596
01:21:48,646 --> 01:21:53,284
After a blastomere is selected, it is then drawn into the pipette.

597
01:21:53,284 --> 01:21:56,009
B. Trophectoderm biopsy.

598
01:21:56,009 --> 01:22:01,196
Reproduced with permission from Doody KJ: Treatment of the infertile couple.

599
01:22:01,196 --> 01:22:08,646
Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

600
01:22:08,646 --> 01:22:12,571
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

601
01:22:12,571 --> 01:22:18,784
PGT-A is a type of aneuploidy screening test that evaluates the number of each chromosome.

602
01:22:18,784 --> 01:22:26,721
Although clearly an appealing prospect, available data are limited, and an optimal testing platform is not yet established.

603
01:22:26,721 --> 01:22:36,171
Importantly, if PGT-A is performed with next-generation sequencing following trophectoderm biopsy, mosaicism is frequently identified .

604
01:22:36,171 --> 01:22:43,096
Despite an increased risk for poor outcome, many such cases have resulted in healthy, normal children .

605
01:22:43,096 --> 01:22:54,771
The American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2018) has concluded that the utility of routine performance of PGT-A for all IVF pregnancies has yet to be determined.

606
01:22:54,771 --> 01:23:02,671
PGT-M may be performed when there is increased risk for a particular single-gene disorder or a hereditary cancer syndrome .

607
01:23:02,671 --> 01:23:06,596
It is a test for a specific condition rather than a panel.

608
01:23:06,596 --> 01:23:15,509
In selected cases, this technique may also be used identify a suitable future donor of umbilical cord blood for an affected family member.

609
01:23:15,509 --> 01:23:26,134
PGT-SR is offered if one of the parents is a carrier of such a rearrangement, such as a duplication, deletion, translocation, insertion, or inversion.

610
01:23:26,134 --> 01:23:32,746
The test will identify an unbalanced chromosomal rearrangement but will not detect a balanced carrier.

611
01:23:32,746 --> 01:23:36,321
Counseling should therefore include this limitation.

